 # 🐧 History of Linux Operating System

## 📖 The Beginning — UNIX (1969)

The history of Linux begins with **UNIX**, which was developed in **1969** at **Bell Labs** by **Ken Thompson** and **Dennis Ritchie**.

UNIX introduced many concepts that Linux still follows today:

- 👥 Multi-user operating system
- 📂 Hierarchical file system
- 📄 Everything is a file
- 🔒 Strong security and permissions
- ⚙️ Portable and modular design

These ideas became the foundation of modern operating systems.

---

📚 MINIX (1987)

In **1987**, **Andrew S. Tanenbaum**, a professor of Computer Science, created **MINIX**.

MINIX was:

- Designed for educational purposes
- UNIX-like operating system
- Used to teach operating system concepts
- Source code available for study

However, its license restricted modification and redistribution, which limited its practical use.

---

## 🚀 Birth of Linux (1991)

In **1991**, **Linus Torvalds**, a **21-year-old Computer Science student** at the **University of Helsinki**, was using MINIX.

He wanted an operating system that he could freely modify and improve.

### 📅 August 25, 1991

Linus posted a famous message on the **comp.os.minix** newsgroup:

> "I'm doing a (free) operating system (just a hobby, won't be big and professional like GNU)."

This announcement marked the beginning of Linux.

### 📅 September 17, 1991

Linux Kernel **Version 0.01** was officially released.

Initially, Linux had its own license.

In **1992**, Linus released Linux under the **GNU General Public License (GPL)**, making it free and open source.

---

## 🛠 Combining with GNU

The **GNU Project** was started by **Richard Stallman** in **1983**.

GNU had already developed:

- GCC Compiler
- Bash Shell
- Core Utilities
- Libraries
- Development Tools

The only missing component was a kernel.

Linux provided that missing kernel.

Together:

```
GNU Tools + Linux Kernel = GNU/Linux Operating System
```

---

## 📈 Growth During the 1990s

Linux quickly became popular.

### 1993

- Slackware released
- Debian released

These became two of the earliest Linux distributions.

### 1994

Linux Kernel **1.0** was officially released.

Developers began creating complete Linux distributions by adding:

- Package Managers
- Desktop Environments
- Installation Tools
- Software Repositories

---

## 🌍 Rise of Linux Distributions (2000s)

Several popular Linux distributions emerged.

| Distribution | First Release | Purpose |
|--------------|--------------|----------|
| Red Hat | 1994 | Enterprise Linux |
| Debian | 1993 | Community-driven Stable OS |
| Ubuntu | 2004 | Beginner-friendly Desktop |
| Fedora | 2003 | Cutting-edge Features |
| openSUSE | 2005 | Developers & Enterprise |
| CentOS | 2004 | Free Enterprise Linux |
| Arch Linux | 2002 | Advanced Users |

Each distribution serves different users and use cases.

---

## ☁ Linux Today

Today Linux powers almost every area of computing.

### 🌐 Servers

Most web servers worldwide run Linux.

### ☁ Cloud Computing

Cloud providers such as:

- AWS
- Google Cloud
- Microsoft Azure

primarily use Linux.

### 🤖 Android

Android is built on the Linux Kernel.

### 🖥 Supercomputers

Nearly **100% of the world's Top 500 supercomputers** run Linux.

### 🏢 Enterprise

Many companies use Linux for:

- Servers
- Containers
- Kubernetes
- Networking
- DevOps

---

## 👨‍💻 Linux Foundation

Today Linux is developed by thousands of developers around the world.

Its development is coordinated by the **Linux Foundation**.

**Linus Torvalds** still actively reviews and maintains the Linux Kernel.

